# J46 — worklist verb + origin restore (keyless)  ⭐ release-blocking
#
# Keyless, deterministic. The `worklist` verb is a contextual-utility full-frame
# deck: opening it captures the launch origin, and Esc restores that exact origin
# (the non-nesting contract). Origin is pinned deterministically with `/setup`
# (which always reopens Setup). The scene deck reads the run-artifact attack
# surface, not the seeded context snapshot (see J43), so with no hydrated run the
# deck honestly shows the `no risk worklist` guidance panel — the verb still lands
# on the worklist page (exempt from the empty-page DFD redirect), never a fabricated
# row. The seeded snapshot is included verbatim from J43 to prove a context snapshot
# alone does not forge deck rows.
#
# Caveat: macOS keychain / OAuth-cached credentials cannot be masked by env-unset;
# this journey needs no credential.
name: the worklist verb opens the deck and Esc restores the exact origin
size: 120x36
cwd: @TMP_TARGET
env-unset: ANTHROPIC_API_KEY OPENAI_API_KEY NO_COLOR
env: HOME=@TMP_HOME KUZUSHI_REDUCED_MOTION=1
# Seed a context snapshot with an attack-surface map (verbatim from J43). The scene
# deck reads the run-artifact surface, not this snapshot, so the deck stays empty.
shell: mkdir -p @TMP_TARGET/.kuzushi && printf '{"attackSurfaceMap":{"rootName":"demo","surfaces":[{"id":"s1","kind":"http_route","path":"a.py","line":3,"language":"python","title":"t1","evidence":"e","risk":{"rank":1,"score":92,"tier":"critical","reasons":["cmd exec"]}},{"id":"s2","kind":"http_route","path":"b.py","line":5,"language":"python","title":"t2","evidence":"e","risk":{"rank":2,"score":70,"tier":"high","reasons":["authz"]}},{"id":"s3","kind":"http_route","path":"c.py","line":9,"language":"python","title":"t3","evidence":"e","risk":{"rank":3,"score":40,"tier":"medium","reasons":[]}}],"summary":{"total":3,"byKind":{},"byFile":{"a.py":1,"b.py":1,"c.py":1},"highRisk":2,"maxRiskScore":92,"byRiskTier":{}}}}' > @TMP_TARGET/.kuzushi/context-snapshot.json
launch: --target @TMP_TARGET
wait: 6
# Pin a deterministic origin: /setup always reopens Setup explicitly.
submit: setup
wait-for: 15 READY THE MAP RUN
snap: origin-setup
expect: READY THE MAP RUN
# The verb opens the full-frame worklist deck (rank rows, or the honest empty
# guidance panel when no run-artifact surface is hydrated).
submit: worklist
wait-for: 15 no risk worklist
snap: worklist-deck
expect: no risk worklist
expect: worklist:rank
# Non-nesting contract: Esc restores the exact origin chrome, no cancellation.
keys: Escape
wait-for: 15 READY THE MAP RUN
snap: origin-restored
expect: READY THE MAP RUN
ask: Opening the worklist deck captured the origin and Esc restored it exactly (Setup's READY THE MAP RUN chrome) — does the contextual-utility non-nesting contract hold, with no nested utility stack and no cancellation of engagement state?
